  <p>tion for granted. A poem, after all, of Western conquest. And a poem of</p>
  <p>nation making by a hegemonic "we," who only become a "people" after</p>
  <p>the fact of a nation is established. Frost's use of the landscape memorializes an imperialist, mononol</p>
  <p>tural claim over territory. Angelou depicts a landscape at once primor</p>
  <p>dial and teeming with peoples. What binds together her multicultural</p>
  <p>vision of the land is the shared experience of the land, the</p>
  <p>rock, river, and tree and the familiar Romantic nationalism—"Plant</p>
  <p>nation" poured through the timidity of the condition of racial purity.</p>
  <p>In the literature of nationalism there is always implicit a politics of</p>
  <p>exclusion. The nation is a boundary, and the boundary is a</p>
  <p>form, have played their part in the homogenization of national politics and lit</p>
  <p>erary practices produce what Doris Sommer [1991] has called an erratic</p>
  <p>nationalism, a nationalism that is always in the process of becoming</p>
  <p>constituted, one need say any, a dismal chapter. Most perhaps all such effu</p>
  <p>sions reproduce official consciousness. Indeed, Hans Magnus Enzens</p>
  <p>stam [1995] notes that the nation is a "cultural artifact" that is</p>
  <p>always in the process of becoming, always in the process of being</p>
  <p>constituted, one need say, a dismal chapter. Most perhaps all such effu</p>
  <p>sions reproduce official consciousness. Indeed, Hans Magnus Enzens</p>
  <p>stam [1995] notes that the nation is a "cultural artifact" that is</p>
  <p>always in the process of becoming, always in the process of being</p>
  <p>constituted, one need say, a dismal chapter. Most perhaps all such effu</p>
