  <p>stam [1995] notes that the nation is a "cultural artifact" that is</p>
  <p>always in the process of becoming, always in the process of being</p>
  <p>constituted, one need say, a dismal chapter. Most perhaps all such effu</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="running-head">Multicultural Nationalism 253</div>
  <div class="page-number">{{ page_number_253 }}</div>
  <p>—its nationalisms require the reconstruction yet again of a transcended</p>
  <p>ing community, a "unity of contraries"—even, in the limiting case, as</p>
  <p>Angelou has it, of "Midas" and the "mendicant." Under</p>
  <p>this, the nation is a "cultural artifact" that is always in the process of</p>
  <p>becoming. The nation is a "cultural artifact" that is always in the process of</p>
  <p>becoming, and the nation is always in the process of being constituted.</p>
  <p>Here on the pulse of this new day</p>
  <p>You may have the grace to look up and out</p>
  <p>And into your sister's eyes and into</p>
  <p>your brother's face.</p>
  <p>—dramatic class, racial/ethnic, and gender inequalities continue to de</p>
  <p>termine different groups' destiny, undermining the hope of this time, a</p>
  <p>multicultural harmony.</p>
  <p>The nation as an imagined community is a "cultural artifact" that</p>
  <p>keeps changing and taking new forms (Anderson 1983: 4). In the case of</p>
  <p>American nationalism, it is necessary to bear in mind the hegemonic</p>
</div>

