  <p>plottation, oppression, and domination to "respect for differences." She</p>
  <p>does not, however, do so in a way that is truly egalitarian. She</p>
  <p>revealed the deep connection between ethnic pluralism and "race" rela</p>
  <p>tions, and the ways in which the nation is constituted by the</p>
  <p>racial and ethnic relations of the people. The nation is not a neutral</p>
  <p>space, but a site of contestation, a site of struggle, a site of</p>
  <p>racial and ethnic relations. The nation is a site where the</p>
  <p>struggle for recognition and inclusion is waged.</p>
